steps.body:
Depth-first search (DFS) goes as deep as it can in one direction before it backs up. On a grid that means: handle this cell, then have the method call itself on the four neighbors. Turn each row into a `char[]` with `toCharArray()` so cells can change, and print a row back with `new String(row)`.

```java
static int fill(char[][] grid, int r, int c) {
    if (r < 0 || r >= grid.length || c < 0 || c >= grid[r].length) {
        return 0;                 // off the grid
    }
    if (grid[r][c] != '.') {
        return 0;                 // wall, or already filled
    }
    grid[r][c] = '*';             // mark before recursing
    return 1 + fill(grid, r + 1, c) + fill(grid, r - 1, c)
            + fill(grid, r, c + 1) + fill(grid, r, c - 1);
}
```

Check bounds first: `||` short-circuits, so `grid[r][c]` is never read off the edge. Mark the cell *before* the recursive calls, or two neighbors call each other until the stack overflows. Each call returns how many cells it filled, so the first call returns the region's size.

Marking changes the grid. When the grid must stay intact, keep a `boolean[][] visited` of the same size and test `visited[r][c]` instead.

