```

Here `dfs` is a flood fill that marks a `boolean[][] visited` (all `false` to start) instead of changing the map, so the map itself stays unchanged. It checks bounds first, skips water and visited cells, marks the cell, and returns the size of its island: 1 for this cell plus what the four neighbors add. To track the largest region, update `largest = Math.max(largest, size)` after each launch.

Only up, down, left, and right connect. Two land cells that touch only at a corner are separate islands. Every cell is scanned once and every land cell is marked once, so the work grows with the size of the grid. The recursion goes as deep as the island is big, so keep islands under about 2,000 cells, the depth limit from the Recursion lesson.

