steps.body:
Backtracking is DFS over choices instead of cells. For subsets, every number has two branches: take it or leave it. Keep the current choices in one `List<Integer> path` and follow **choose, explore, unchoose**:

```java
static void subsets(int[] nums, int i, List<Integer> path, List<List<Integer>> results) {
    if (i == nums.length) {
        results.add(new ArrayList<>(path));   // save a copy
        return;
    }
    path.add(nums[i]);                        // choose
    subsets(nums, i + 1, path, results);      // explore with it
    path.remove(path.size() - 1);             // unchoose
    subsets(nums, i + 1, path, results);      // explore without it
}
```

Taking a number before skipping it gives include-first order: the full set comes out first and the empty set `[]` last.

Two gotchas. `results.add(path)` stores a reference to the one shared list, which is empty again when the search ends, so every result would print `[]`. Copy it with `new ArrayList<>(path)`. And `path.remove(path.size() - 1)` removes by index because its argument is an `int`. Undoing the choice is what lets one list serve every branch. With n numbers there are 2 to the n subsets (8 for 3 numbers), so keep n small.

