steps.body:
A permutation uses every item once, in some order. At each position, try every item the path does not hold yet. A `boolean[] used` remembers which ones those are:

```java
static void permute(char[] letters, boolean[] used, StringBuilder sb, List<String> results) {
    if (sb.length() == letters.length) {
        results.add(sb.toString());
        return;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < letters.length; i++) {
        if (used[i]) {
            continue;
        }
        used[i] = true;                        // choose
        sb.append(letters[i]);
        permute(letters, used, sb, results);   // explore
        sb.deleteCharAt(sb.length() - 1);      // unchoose
        used[i] = false;
    }
}
```

The loop tries items left to right, so a sorted input gives permutations in lexicographic order. Sort first with `Arrays.sort(letters)` or `Arrays.sort(names)`. Undo both the builder and `used[i]`, or later branches see stale choices.

For whole words, use a `List<String> path` with `add` and `path.remove(path.size() - 1)` instead of a `StringBuilder`. `String.join(" -> ", path)` joins any list of strings. n items have n! orderings (24 for 4), so keep inputs tiny. Repeated items would print repeated permutations, so these inputs are distinct.

